WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.617
Aliases and grain solve two different kinds of confusion.

00:00:03.617 --> 00:00:06.787
Aliases make the SQL easier to read.

00:00:06.787 --> 00:00:09.272
If both patients and admissions contain patient_id,

00:00:09.272 --> 00:00:15.542
writing p.patient_id and a.patient_id tells us exactly where the column comes from.

00:00:15.542 --> 00:00:20.402
As queries grow to three, four, or five tables, aliases become essential.

00:00:20.402 --> 00:00:22.212
Grain helps us interpret the result.

00:00:22.212 --> 00:00:24.637
Suppose Alice has two admissions.

00:00:24.637 --> 00:00:30.312
Her patient information may appear twice after the join.

00:00:30.312 --> 00:00:34.027
That does not mean the data is duplicated incorrectly.

00:00:34.027 --> 00:00:39.282
It means each result row represents one admission with patient information attached.

00:00:39.282 --> 00:00:45.157
This is a very important habit: when you see repeated values after a join, do not immediately add DISTINCT.

00:00:45.157 --> 00:00:50.212
DISTINCT can hide a real modeling issue.

00:00:50.212 --> 00:00:53.177
First ask whether the repeated rows represent different valid events.

00:00:53.177 --> 00:00:59.127
For example, if a customer places five orders, their name will appear on five order rows.

00:00:59.127 --> 00:01:06.782
If you use DISTINCT carelessly, you may accidentally remove meaningful detail.

00:01:06.782 --> 00:01:12.702
A good practice is to write one sentence before the query: one row in this result represents one admission

00:01:12.702 --> 00:01:17.392
with patient information. That sentence becomes your validation target.

00:01:17.392 --> 00:01:21.812
If the output behaves differently, you know where to investigate.
